    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-49550",
      "knowledge_point_id": "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent",
      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{1}{25+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{25+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{25\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/25)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{5})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{5}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=5\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{25+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/5)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (5\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
