      "worked_solution": "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $25{x}^{2}=(5x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5x)}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
