      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent",
      "problem": "$\\int \\frac{2}{64+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{64+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2}{64\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/64)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{64}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/64}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/8)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{8})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{8}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{8}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=8\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{64+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{32}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/8)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (8\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{8})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
