      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{9}{81+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{81+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{9}{81\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/81)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/81}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/9)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\n The only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{9})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{9}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{9}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=9\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{81+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/9)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (9\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{9})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
